The bad-character table records the last position of each pattern character. On a mismatch, the pattern can usually move farther than one position. After a complete match, the shift is kept at one position so overlapping matches are not lost.

This is a Boyer-Moore style scan. It is often quick for longer patterns, although its worst-case time is still quadratic.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Solution {
    public List<int> Search(string text, string pattern) {
        List<int> matches = new List<int>();

        if (pattern.Length == 0) {
            for (int i = 0; i <= text.Length; i++) {
                matches.Add(i);
            }
            return matches;
        }

        int[] last = new int[char.MaxValue + 1];
        Array.Fill(last, -1);

        for (int i = 0; i < pattern.Length; i++) {
            last[pattern[i]] = i;
        }

        int start = 0;
        while (start <= text.Length - pattern.Length) {
            int j = pattern.Length - 1;

            while (j >= 0 && pattern[j] == text[start + j]) {
                j--;
            }

            if (j < 0) {
                matches.Add(start);
                start++;
            } else {
                int previous = last[text[start + j]];
                int shift = j - previous;
                start += Math.Max(1, shift);
            }
        }

        return matches;
    }
}
```

Dry run with `text = "abcabc"` and `pattern = "abc"`: the first alignment matches at `0`. After shifting by one, the scan reaches the next valid alignment at `3`, so the result is `[0, 3]`.

The empty pattern case returns all insertion positions. No input string is modified.

Time complexity: average case is often close to `O(n)`, while the worst case is `O(nm)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` for the fixed character table, not counting the output list.

The pattern and text are joined with a separator, then the Z array is built. A Z value tells how many characters from the current position match the start of the joined string. When that value reaches the pattern length, a match starts in the text.

The separator is stored as a character that cannot match normal input characters. The original text is not changed.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    public:
       vector<int> search(string text , string pattern){
           vector<int> answer;
           if (pattern.empty()) {
               for (int i = 0; i <= static_cast<int>(text.size()); ++i) {
                   answer.push_back(i);
               }
               return answer;
           }

           string combined = pattern;
           combined.push_back('#');
           combined += text;

           int n = combined.size();
           vector<int> z(n, 0);
           int left = 0;
           int right = 0;

           for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
               if (i <= right) {
                   z[i] = min(right - i + 1, z[i - left]);
               }

               while (i + z[i] < n &&
                      combined[z[i]] == combined[i + z[i]]) {
                   ++z[i];
               }

               if (i + z[i] - 1 > right) {
                   left = i;
                   right = i + z[i] - 1;
               }

               if (z[i] == static_cast<int>(pattern.size())) {
                   answer.push_back(i - pattern.size() - 1);
               }
           }

           return answer;
       }
};
```

For example, with `text = "ababa"` and `pattern = "aba"`, matches are found at `0` and `2`. The Z values at the matching positions are both `3`.

Time complexity: `O(text length + pattern length)`.
Space complexity: `O(text length + pattern length)`.

KMP keeps a prefix table for the pattern. The table says where to continue when a mismatch happens, so already checked text characters do not need to be checked again.

After a full match, the pattern index moves using the prefix table instead of going back to zero. That is what allows overlapping matches such as `"aa"` inside `"aaaa"`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> search(String text, String pattern) {
        List<Integer> matches = new ArrayList<>();
        if (pattern.isEmpty()) {
            for (int i = 0; i <= text.length(); i++) {
                matches.add(i);
            }
            return matches;
        }

        int[] prefix = new int[pattern.length()];
        for (int i = 1, length = 0; i < pattern.length();) {
            if (pattern.charAt(i) == pattern.charAt(length)) {
                prefix[i++] = ++length;
            } else if (length > 0) {
                length = prefix[length - 1];
            } else {
                prefix[i++] = 0;
            }
        }

        int textIndex = 0;
        int patternIndex = 0;
        while (textIndex < text.length()) {
            if (text.charAt(textIndex) == pattern.charAt(patternIndex)) {
                textIndex++;
                patternIndex++;

                if (patternIndex == pattern.length()) {
                    matches.add(textIndex - pattern.length());
                    patternIndex = prefix[patternIndex - 1];
                }
            } else if (patternIndex > 0) {
                patternIndex = prefix[patternIndex - 1];
            } else {
                textIndex++;
            }
        }

        return matches;
    }
}
```

An empty pattern is treated as matching at every position from `0` through `text.length()`. The input strings stay unchanged.

Time: `O(text length + pattern length)`.
Space: `O(pattern length)` for the prefix table.

A direct scan is useful when the input is small or when the easiest code to verify is preferred. Each possible starting position is checked with an inner loop, and a match is recorded without skipping ahead.

Because the outer loop moves by one, overlapping results are kept.

```javascript
class Solution {
    search(text, pattern) {
        const matches = [];

        if (pattern.length === 0) {
            for (let i = 0; i <= text.length; i++) {
                matches.push(i);
            }
            return matches;
        }

        for (let start = 0; start + pattern.length <= text.length; start++) {
            let matched = true;

            for (let j = 0; j < pattern.length; j++) {
                if (text[start + j] !== pattern[j]) {
                    matched = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (matched) {
                matches.push(start);
            }
        }

        return matches;
    }
}
```

This does not change either input. It also works when the pattern has one character or when the text and pattern are exactly equal.

Time complexity: `O((n - m + 1) * m)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the returned array.

